      "notes": "All claims are well-supported by their cited evidence. Tier assignments are appropriate: measurable claims draw on ONS government data, projected claims are correctly labelled and counter-evidence is presented. The E20 (Pinsent Masons) and E19 (Aon) sources are advocacy/commercial but are used only as corroboration for the 'modest effect' narrative, not as sole basis for the verdict. The direction 'improves' with 'minor' magnitude and 'low' confidence is consistent with the evidence: real gaps documented, small analogue effect from gender reporting, genuine uncertainty about enforcement. The Danish complication (E22) is fairly presented as a caveat rather than suppressed. No motive attribution or party identity language. No false balance \u2014 the mixed evidence on reporting effectiveness is genuine, but the verdict correctly resolves to 'improves/minor' rather than hiding behind uncertainty. The 'push for' framing caveat is noted honestly. No lever leakage detected \u2014 the verdict judges distributional effect, not the policy instrument itself."
